IMPORT prelude
IMPORT types

-- Online Safety (Minimum Age and Child Safety Risk Assessment) Bill (NZ)
-- Part 3 Subpart 3 -- Court-imposed remedies (cll 34 to 56).
-- Source: registers/source-bundle/LMS1592864.xml, 2026 No 339-1, retrieved 2026-09-11.
--
-- What is encoded here: the cl 34 gateway, the cl 35 and cl 38 grounds for
-- restriction orders, the cl 44 pecuniary penalty order with its cl 44(3)
-- defences, the cll 45 to 47 tier partition, and the cll 54 to 56 multiplicity
-- constraints. What is not encoded, and why, is set out under each heading and
-- in NOTES.md section 4: cll 36, 37, 39, 40, 42 and 43 are procedural, and
-- cll 41, 48 and 49 to 53 confer or describe discretion without conditioning it.

§ `Part 3 Subpart 3 -- Court-imposed remedies`

§§ `34. Court-imposed remedies available under this subpart`

DECLARE `remedy kind` IS ONE OF
    `a pecuniary penalty order`
    `a restriction order`
    `an injunction`

-- cl 34(1) and (2): the three remedies are available for a specified liability
-- act. The nine acts themselves are enumerated in types.l4 from cl 34(3).

GIVEN k IS A `remedy kind`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the remedy is available under this subpart` IF
    k EQUALS k

§§ `35. Service restriction orders`

DECLARE `restriction order application` HAS
    name                                                                                IS A STRING
    -- cl 35(2)(a) and (b), cl 38(2)(a) and (b)
    `the operator has carried out a specified liability act`                             IS A BOOLEAN
    `the act is ongoing`                                                                 IS A BOOLEAN
    -- cl 35(2)(c)(i)(A) to (D)
    `the operator failed to comply with a requirement to disclose a warning`             IS A BOOLEAN
    `the operator breached an undertaking accepted by the regulator`                      IS A BOOLEAN
    `the operator failed to comply with a corrective notice`                              IS A BOOLEAN
    `the operator failed to comply with a pecuniary penalty order`                        IS A BOOLEAN
    -- cl 35(2)(c)(ii)
    `the circumstances or the risk are such that the order is appropriate`                IS A BOOLEAN
    -- cl 35(2)(d)
    `the ancillary service provider provides that service for the platform`               IS A BOOLEAN
    -- cl 38(2)(c)(i) and (ii)
    `a service restriction order has been made and is not sufficient`                     IS A BOOLEAN
    `a service restriction order would be unlikely to be sufficient`                      IS A BOOLEAN
    -- cl 38(2)(d) and (e)
    `the access service provider provides that service for the platform`                  IS A BOOLEAN
    `the order is appropriate and its restrictions are proportionate to that purpose`     IS A BOOLEAN

-- cl 35(2)(c)(i): any of the four non-compliance limbs.
GIVEN a IS A `restriction order application`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the operator has failed to respond to earlier enforcement` IF
        a's `the operator failed to comply with a requirement to disclose a warning`
    OR  a's `the operator breached an undertaking accepted by the regulator`
    OR  a's `the operator failed to comply with a corrective notice`
    OR  a's `the operator failed to comply with a pecuniary penalty order`

@ref Online Safety (Minimum Age and Child Safety Risk Assessment) Bill (NZ) cl 35(2)
@export Decide whether the High Court may make a service restriction order
GIVEN a IS A `restriction order application` @desc The application: whether a specified liability act was carried out and is ongoing, whether the operator failed to respond to earlier enforcement or the circumstances otherwise make an order appropriate, and whether the ancillary service provider provides that service for the platform.
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the court may make a service restriction order` IF
        a's `the operator has carried out a specified liability act`
    AND a's `the act is ongoing`
    AND (    `the operator has failed to respond to earlier enforcement` a
          OR a's `the circumstances or the risk are such that the order is appropriate` )
    AND a's `the ancillary service provider provides that service for the platform`

§§ `38. Access restriction orders`

-- cl 38(2)(c): either a service restriction order has been made and is not
-- sufficient, or the grounds for one are met but it would be unlikely to
-- suffice. Limb (ii)(A) requires the SERVICE restriction grounds, which is why
-- the rule below calls back into cl 35.

GIVEN a IS A `restriction order application`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the service restriction route is inadequate` IF
        a's `a service restriction order has been made and is not sufficient`
    OR  (    `the court may make a service restriction order` a
          AND a's `a service restriction order would be unlikely to be sufficient` )

@ref Online Safety (Minimum Age and Child Safety Risk Assessment) Bill (NZ) cl 38(2)
@export Decide whether the High Court may make an access restriction order
GIVEN a IS A `restriction order application` @desc The application, plus whether a service restriction order has been made and is insufficient or would be unlikely to suffice, whether the access service provider provides that service for the platform, and whether the order is appropriate and proportionate.
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the court may make an access restriction order` IF
        a's `the operator has carried out a specified liability act`
    AND a's `the act is ongoing`
    AND `the service restriction route is inadequate` a
    AND a's `the access service provider provides that service for the platform`
    AND a's `the order is appropriate and its restrictions are proportionate to that purpose`

§§ `36, 37, 39, 40, 41, 42, 43 -- not encoded`

-- cll 36 and 39 (interim orders) turn on the court's opinion that an interim
-- order is desirable; cll 37 and 40 prescribe what an order must contain;
-- cl 41(1) requires the court to consider named persons' rights without
-- conditioning the outcome on them; cl 41(2) is a notification duty on the
-- regulator; cl 42 is renewal procedure; cl 43 is an appeal right on a question
-- of law. None of them states a test an encoding can decide.

§§ `44. Pecuniary penalty order`

DECLARE `pecuniary penalty application` HAS
    name                                                                                IS A STRING
    -- cl 44(2)
    `the court is satisfied the operator carried out a specified liability act`          IS A BOOLEAN
    -- cl 44(3)(a)(i)
    `the conduct occurred due to an act or omission of another beyond the operator's control` IS A BOOLEAN
    -- cl 44(3)(a)(ii)
    `the operator reasonably relied on information supplied by an outside person`        IS A BOOLEAN
    -- cl 44(3)(b)
    `the operator took steps that were reasonable in the circumstances to prevent the act` IS A BOOLEAN

-- cl 44(3): the court MUST NOT make the order if the operator satisfies the
-- court of any one of the three matters. The burden is on the operator.
GIVEN a IS A `pecuniary penalty application`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the operator makes out a section 44(3) defence` IF
        a's `the conduct occurred due to an act or omission of another beyond the operator's control`
    OR  a's `the operator reasonably relied on information supplied by an outside person`
    OR  a's `the operator took steps that were reasonable in the circumstances to prevent the act`

@ref Online Safety (Minimum Age and Child Safety Risk Assessment) Bill (NZ) cl 44
@export Decide whether the High Court may make a pecuniary penalty order
GIVEN a IS A `pecuniary penalty application` @desc Whether the court is satisfied the operator carried out a specified liability act, and whether the operator makes out any of the three defences in cl 44(3).
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the court may make a pecuniary penalty order` IF
        a's `the court is satisfied the operator carried out a specified liability act`
    AND NOT `the operator makes out a section 44(3) defence` a

§§ `45, 46, 47. Maximum penalty by tier`

-- cl 45 applies to cl 34(3)(a) to (c), cl 46 to (d) to (g), cl 47 to (h) and (i).
-- Encoding the routing as a total function over the enum is what makes the
-- partition checkable: the typechecker rejects an unhandled act, and the cases
-- below pin each act to the tier the Bill assigns it. See NOTES.md, probe 2.

GIVEN act IS A `specified liability act`
GIVETH A `penalty tier`
DECIDE `the tier for` act IS
    CONSIDER act
    WHEN `section 34(3)(a) -- failure to take reasonable steps under section 11`                   THEN `tier 1`
    WHEN `section 34(3)(b) -- keeping personal information contrary to section 12(1)(a)`           THEN `tier 1`
    WHEN `section 34(3)(c) -- using or disclosing personal information contrary to section 12(1)(b)` THEN `tier 1`
    WHEN `section 34(3)(d) -- inadequate assessment on a directed repeat under section 19`         THEN `tier 2`
    WHEN `section 34(3)(e) -- failure to comply with a direction to repeat under section 19`       THEN `tier 2`
    WHEN `section 34(3)(f) -- failure to provide a risk assessment to the regulator`               THEN `tier 2`
    WHEN `section 34(3)(g) -- failure to designate a responsible individual under section 18`      THEN `tier 2`
    WHEN `section 34(3)(h) -- failure to comply with a notice to disclose a warning`               THEN `tier 3`
    WHEN `section 34(3)(i) -- failure to comply with a corrective notice`                          THEN `tier 3`

`tier 1 maximum for an individual` MEANS   500000
`tier 2 maximum for an individual` MEANS   250000
`tier 3 maximum for an individual` MEANS   100000

`tier 1 fixed maximum` MEANS 40000000
`tier 2 fixed maximum` MEANS 12000000
`tier 3 fixed maximum` MEANS  4000000

`tier 1 turnover fraction` MEANS 0.10
`tier 2 turnover fraction` MEANS 0.03
`tier 3 turnover fraction` MEANS 0.01

GIVEN x IS A NUMBER, y IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `the greater of` x y IS
    IF x AT LEAST y THEN x ELSE y

DECLARE `penalty exposure` HAS
    name                                        IS A STRING
    `the act`                                   IS A `specified liability act`
    `the operator is an individual`             IS A BOOLEAN
    `relevant global turnover in dollars`       IS A NUMBER

-- cll 45(2), 46(2) and 47(2): an individual has a flat maximum; anyone else
-- faces the greater of the fixed sum and a percentage of relevant global
-- turnover for each financial year in which the act occurred. The encoding
-- takes a single year's turnover as supplied.

@ref Online Safety (Minimum Age and Child Safety Risk Assessment) Bill (NZ) cll 45, 46, 47
@export Compute the maximum pecuniary penalty for a specified liability act
GIVEN e IS A `penalty exposure` @desc Which specified liability act was carried out, whether the operator is an individual, and the operator's relevant global turnover in dollars for the financial year in which the act occurred.
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `the maximum pecuniary penalty for` e IS
    CONSIDER `the tier for` (e's `the act`)
    WHEN `tier 1` THEN IF   e's `the operator is an individual`
                       THEN `tier 1 maximum for an individual`
                       ELSE `the greater of` `tier 1 fixed maximum`
                                             (`tier 1 turnover fraction` * e's `relevant global turnover in dollars`)
    WHEN `tier 2` THEN IF   e's `the operator is an individual`
                       THEN `tier 2 maximum for an individual`
                       ELSE `the greater of` `tier 2 fixed maximum`
                                             (`tier 2 turnover fraction` * e's `relevant global turnover in dollars`)
    WHEN `tier 3` THEN IF   e's `the operator is an individual`
                       THEN `tier 3 maximum for an individual`
                       ELSE `the greater of` `tier 3 fixed maximum`
                                             (`tier 3 turnover fraction` * e's `relevant global turnover in dollars`)

§§ `48, 49, 50, 51, 52, 53 -- not encoded`

-- cl 48 lists matters the court must have regard to in fixing a penalty within
-- the maximum; it constrains reasoning, not outcome. cll 49 to 51 confer
-- injunction powers on the court's satisfaction or opinion, cl 52 removes the
-- requirement for an undertaking as to damages, and cl 53 applies the ordinary
-- civil rules and standard of proof. None yields a decidable test.

§§ `54, 55, 56. Multiplicity of remedies`

DECLARE `enforcement history` HAS
    name                                                                                IS A STRING
    `the conduct constitutes 2 or more specified liability acts`                         IS A BOOLEAN
    `a pecuniary penalty order has already been made for the same conduct`               IS A BOOLEAN
    `a restriction order has already been made for the same conduct`                     IS A BOOLEAN
    `an injunction has already been granted for the same conduct`                        IS A BOOLEAN
    `the person is liable to a fine or imprisonment under any Act for the same conduct`  IS A BOOLEAN

-- cl 54: a remedy of one kind may be granted even though a remedy of a
-- DIFFERENT kind has been granted for the same conduct.
@ref Online Safety (Minimum Age and Child Safety Risk Assessment) Bill (NZ) cl 54
GIVEN h IS A `enforcement history`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a restriction order may still be made despite an earlier remedy of a different kind` IF
        h's `a pecuniary penalty order has already been made for the same conduct`
    OR  h's `an injunction has already been granted for the same conduct`
    OR  NOT h's `a restriction order has already been made for the same conduct`

-- cl 55(b): no person is liable to more than 1 pecuniary penalty order for the
-- same conduct, even where the conduct constitutes two or more specified
-- liability acts (cl 55(a) allows proceeding on any one or more of them).
-- cl 56: no pecuniary penalty and criminal penalty for the same conduct.
@ref Online Safety (Minimum Age and Child Safety Risk Assessment) Bill (NZ) cll 55, 56
@export Decide whether a further pecuniary penalty order may be made for the same conduct
GIVEN h IS A `enforcement history` @desc What has already been imposed for this conduct: an earlier pecuniary penalty order, a restriction order, an injunction, or a fine or term of imprisonment under any Act.
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a further pecuniary penalty order may be made for the same conduct` IF
        NOT h's `a pecuniary penalty order has already been made for the same conduct`
    AND NOT h's `the person is liable to a fine or imprisonment under any Act for the same conduct`

§§ `Scenario fixtures`

`a tier 1 body corporate` MEANS `penalty exposure` WITH
    name                                    IS "large platform, section 11 failure"
    `the act`                               IS `section 34(3)(a) -- failure to take reasonable steps under section 11`
    `the operator is an individual`         IS FALSE
    `relevant global turnover in dollars`   IS 800000000

`a tier 1 small body corporate` MEANS `penalty exposure` WITH
    name                                    IS "small platform, section 11 failure"
    `the act`                               IS `section 34(3)(a) -- failure to take reasonable steps under section 11`
    `the operator is an individual`         IS FALSE
    `relevant global turnover in dollars`   IS 50000000

`a tier 2 individual` MEANS `penalty exposure` WITH
    name                                    IS "individual operator, late risk assessment"
    `the act`                               IS `section 34(3)(f) -- failure to provide a risk assessment to the regulator`
    `the operator is an individual`         IS TRUE
    `relevant global turnover in dollars`   IS 0

`a tier 3 body corporate` MEANS `penalty exposure` WITH
    name                                    IS "platform ignoring a corrective notice"
    `the act`                               IS `section 34(3)(i) -- failure to comply with a corrective notice`
    `the operator is an individual`         IS FALSE
    `relevant global turnover in dollars`   IS 800000000

`an operator already penalised` MEANS `enforcement history` WITH
    name                                                                                IS "second application on the same conduct"
    `the conduct constitutes 2 or more specified liability acts`                         IS TRUE
    `a pecuniary penalty order has already been made for the same conduct`               IS TRUE
    `a restriction order has already been made for the same conduct`                     IS FALSE
    `an injunction has already been granted for the same conduct`                        IS FALSE
    `the person is liable to a fine or imprisonment under any Act for the same conduct`  IS FALSE

`an operator already fined` MEANS `enforcement history` WITH
    name                                                                                IS "criminal fine already imposed for the same conduct"
    `the conduct constitutes 2 or more specified liability acts`                         IS FALSE
    `a pecuniary penalty order has already been made for the same conduct`               IS FALSE
    `a restriction order has already been made for the same conduct`                     IS FALSE
    `an injunction has already been granted for the same conduct`                        IS FALSE
    `the person is liable to a fine or imprisonment under any Act for the same conduct`  IS TRUE

`an operator relying on a third party` MEANS `pecuniary penalty application` WITH
    name                                                                                IS "age assurance vendor failure"
    `the court is satisfied the operator carried out a specified liability act`          IS TRUE
    `the conduct occurred due to an act or omission of another beyond the operator's control` IS TRUE
    `the operator reasonably relied on information supplied by an outside person`        IS FALSE
    `the operator took steps that were reasonable in the circumstances to prevent the act` IS FALSE

§§ `Machine-evaluated cases`

-- LQA probe 2: the tier partition. Encoding cll 45 to 47 as a total function
-- over the nine specified liability acts means the typechecker rejects any act
-- the Bill does not route, and these cases pin each act to its tier. The
-- partition is total and disjoint on the text as drafted.
#ASSERT `the tier for` `section 34(3)(a) -- failure to take reasonable steps under section 11` EQUALS `tier 1`
#ASSERT `the tier for` `section 34(3)(b) -- keeping personal information contrary to section 12(1)(a)` EQUALS `tier 1`
#ASSERT `the tier for` `section 34(3)(c) -- using or disclosing personal information contrary to section 12(1)(b)` EQUALS `tier 1`
#ASSERT `the tier for` `section 34(3)(d) -- inadequate assessment on a directed repeat under section 19` EQUALS `tier 2`
#ASSERT `the tier for` `section 34(3)(e) -- failure to comply with a direction to repeat under section 19` EQUALS `tier 2`
#ASSERT `the tier for` `section 34(3)(f) -- failure to provide a risk assessment to the regulator` EQUALS `tier 2`
#ASSERT `the tier for` `section 34(3)(g) -- failure to designate a responsible individual under section 18` EQUALS `tier 2`
#ASSERT `the tier for` `section 34(3)(h) -- failure to comply with a notice to disclose a warning` EQUALS `tier 3`
#ASSERT `the tier for` `section 34(3)(i) -- failure to comply with a corrective notice` EQUALS `tier 3`

-- cl 45(2)(b): the greater of $40m and 10% of relevant global turnover. On
-- $800m turnover the percentage limb governs at $80m; on $50m turnover the
-- fixed limb governs at $40m.
#EVAL `the maximum pecuniary penalty for` `a tier 1 body corporate`
#EVAL `the maximum pecuniary penalty for` `a tier 1 small body corporate`
#ASSERT `the maximum pecuniary penalty for` `a tier 1 body corporate` EQUALS 80000000
#ASSERT `the maximum pecuniary penalty for` `a tier 1 small body corporate` EQUALS 40000000

-- cl 46(2)(a): an individual faces the flat figure, whatever the turnover.
#ASSERT `the maximum pecuniary penalty for` `a tier 2 individual` EQUALS 250000

-- cl 47(2)(b): 1% of $800m is $8m, which exceeds the $4m fixed limb.
#ASSERT `the maximum pecuniary penalty for` `a tier 3 body corporate` EQUALS 8000000

-- cl 55(b) and cl 56: one pecuniary penalty order per course of conduct, and
-- never alongside a criminal penalty for the same conduct.
#ASSERT `a further pecuniary penalty order may be made for the same conduct` `an operator already penalised` EQUALS FALSE
#ASSERT `a further pecuniary penalty order may be made for the same conduct` `an operator already fined` EQUALS FALSE

-- cl 44(3)(a)(i): the defence is made out, so the court must not make the order.
#ASSERT `the operator makes out a section 44(3) defence` `an operator relying on a third party` EQUALS TRUE
#ASSERT `the court may make a pecuniary penalty order` `an operator relying on a third party` EQUALS FALSE
